// GitHub issue #462: the second lock class, and the first lock in this
// kernel that does not mask interrupts.
//
// kernel/lib/mutex.tkb masks DAIF.I on every acquire, which is what makes
// a Mutex safe to take from an interrupt handler -- a handler can never
// interrupt a holder on the same core. It is also what makes it wrong for
// a lock held across real work: masking is all-or-nothing, so the whole
// kernel's interrupt latency becomes the longest critical section anyone
// writes.
//
// A TcpConnection is held across a user copy AND a frame transmit
// (kernel_tcp_send_received takes the TX permission). Masking there is the
// same defect issue #452 had to restructure the retransmit path to avoid,
// and here it cannot be restructured away, because the transmit is what
// the syscall is for.
//
// So this class trades the property it does not need for the one it does:
// **no interrupt handler may take a TaskMutex**, and in exchange holding
// one costs no interrupt latency at all. GitHub issue #449 decided that
// whether a handler may take a lock is a property OF THE LOCK, declared
// where the lock is; a separate type is that declaration, and reaching for
// the wrong acquire is not expressible rather than merely wrong.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-28). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - THE INTERRUPT RULE IS CHECKED, but only for the BLOCKING acquire.
//   task_mutex_acquire is `!{may_block}`, so an `!{interrupt}` function
//   that reaches it -- through any depth of calls -- is a compile error
//   naming the path. task_mutex_trylock is not, because it does not spin
//   and a handler using it correctly is a real pattern. What that leaves
//   uncovered: a handler that trylocks in a loop, which is the blocking
//   acquire written out longhand and which nothing here can tell from a
//   handler that gives up.
// - NO OWNER, NO RECURSION COUNT, NO DEADLOCK DETECTION -- same as Mutex.
//   Taking the same one twice on one core hangs.
// - NO LEVEL, and unlike Mutex this one will never need one. A level
//   exists to mask fewer interrupts; this masks none.
// - THE RELEASE IS THE CALLER'S OBLIGATION, and nothing here makes it
//   mandatory. What makes it mandatory at the one call site that exists is
//   that the caller holds a LINEAR token minted only by the acquire --
//   see TcpConnectionOwner, which is the whole reason this class has no
//   guard type of its own.
use "kernel/lib/spinlock.tkb";

// One field, at offset 0, for the same reason Mutex has one: `m as usize`
// is the address the atomic operates on only because the word is first.
struct TaskMutex { private word: usize; }

// Free. Call once, before it is reachable by anything else.
fn task_mutex_init(m: *TaskMutex @ id) {
    static_assert(offsetof(TaskMutex, word) == 0,
                  "TaskMutex.word must be first: `m as usize` is the atomic's address");
    m.word = 0;
}

// Takes it. No mask, so nothing to hand back -- which is why the caller's
// token needs no flags field and why this pair is not shaped like Mutex's.
//
// `may_block` is not decoration and not an approximation. From an
// interrupt handler this genuinely blocks forever: the handler spins for a
// holder that cannot run until the handler returns. The effect system
// already rejects `may_block` on an `!{interrupt}` function and reports
// the call path, so the rule this file's header states is now checked
// rather than asserted -- which is what GitHub issue #449 owed it.
//
// kernel/lib/intrusive_pool.tkb had already worked out that this is what
// the annotation would do, and declined it there because a pool lock IS
// safe from a handler. This is the case it declined it FOR.
fn task_mutex_acquire(m: *TaskMutex @ id) !{locks, may_block, unsafe} {
    spin_lock(m as usize);
}

fn task_mutex_release(m: *TaskMutex @ id) !{locks, unsafe} {
    spin_unlock(m as usize);
}

// One attempt. For a caller with something else to do rather than wait,
// and the only way to observe the exclusion with one thread.
fn task_mutex_trylock(m: *TaskMutex @ id) -> bool !{locks, unsafe} {
    return spin_trylock(m as usize);
}

fn task_mutex_is_held(m: *TaskMutex @ id) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return spin_lock_is_held(m as usize);
}
